From 220a9ef1d71b2f553d8f647faba9adea248889d1 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Fri, 20 Nov 2026 06:10:02 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] run a filter again when an item's size or date changes, and
 on refresh

A Nushell filter's answer was kept for as long as the rows it was made
for had the same paths in the same order. A file that another program
saved comes back under the path it had, so it stayed hidden when it had
grown past `size > 10kb`, and stayed listed, and counted in the status
bar, when it had shrunk under it. A folder's size comes from the index
a moment after the folder it is in has been listed, which is when the
filter is first given the items. In a folder opened with a filter
already on, the folders were therefore judged without a size and not
again: `size > 100mb` listed none of them, and a comparison joined to
another condition, as in `kind == Folder and size > 1mb`, ended in
nu's error for an operator given nothing, which stayed up over an
unfiltered list. Refresh read the folder again and left the answer as
it was. Short of other items being listed, or the same in another
order, only editing the filter's text ran it again.

The rows a filter was run over are now remembered with the size and
date it was given for each, and a run starts when any of that differs:
other items, another order, or an item that came back with another
size or date. Items that come back as they were start none, however
often their folder is looked at or read, as each run starts a `nu`.
Opening a folder with a filter on does cost a second run now when the
folder holds folders the index has sizes for: one over the items as
they are listed, and one more when the sizes are in. Refresh forgets
what the filter was run over, so it runs again for the rows as they
come back. That is also how a filter that goes by more than its rows,
the time of day or what a file says, catches up. The answer before
stays in force until the next one is in, as it does when an item comes
or goes.

Recents and the results of a search by name were held against their
lists on screen by path alone as well, in what is not released yet.
Recents, read again for what the index reported, was shown only if its
paths or their order differed. A file saved where it was, still the
newest, kept the size and date its row had, and its preview with them.
A search run again quietly showed its answer only where the reported
files showed in it, so an answer that came out as the same list left a
saved file's old row too. Both now also show an answer that lists the
same items in the same order when one of them has another size or
date, and that counts as a change for how closely the list is followed
afterwards: a file saved again and again shows each time as soon as
the first. Nothing reshuffles by it: an answer in another order is
still held to what the reports account for. What makes either list be
made again is as it was. Recents is not read for its newest file being
written to again, and saving a file that a search lists does not by
itself run the search.

One test went by the old answer: with `size > 10kb` on and the window
gone up to a folder, it expected the file of 20 KB there and not the
folder beside it that holds one. It expects both now.

Changelog (Fixed): A Nushell filter kept its answer for an item after
the item had changed. A file that another program saved stayed hidden
when it had grown past `size > 10kb`, and stayed listed, and counted in
the status bar, when it had shrunk under it. In a folder opened with a
filter already on, the folders were judged before their sizes were
known, so `size > 100mb` listed none of them, and a comparison joined
to another condition, such as `kind == Folder and size > 1mb`, left a
Nushell error over an unfiltered list. Refresh (Cmd+R) changed none of
it. A filter now runs again when an item comes back with another size
or date, and Refresh runs it again in any case, as a filter that goes
by the time of day needs.

